2027-01-04T13:43:35  thought   A straightforward useful answer. Five minutes, neck-focused, safe. I'll note briefly that if there's numbness or pain down the arm it's a different thing.

2027-01-04T13:43:35  message   To the owner:
Five minutes, all gentle — nothing here should hurt, mild stretch only. Do them seated or standing, whichever's easier.

0:00–0:45 — Warm up. Slow shoulder rolls, 10 back, 10 forward. Then shrug shoulders to your ears and drop them, 10 times.

0:45–1:30 — Chin tucks. Sit tall, pull your chin straight back like you're making a double chin (not looking down). Hold 5 seconds, 8 reps. This one does most of the work for a stiff neck.

1:30–2:30 — Side bend. Right ear toward right shoulder, hold 20 seconds, let the left shoulder stay down. Then left side. Two rounds each. Don't pull on your head with your hand.

2:30–3:30 — Turn and look. Slowly turn your head right as far as is comfortable, hold 10 seconds, back to centre, then left. Three each side. Add a small look-down-diagonally at the end of each turn.

3:30–4:15 — Chest opener. Hands behind your back or on a door frame, gentle squeeze of the shoulder blades, hold 20 seconds, twice. Tight chest pulls the neck forward.

4:15–5:00 — Finish. Shoulder blade squeezes, 10 slow reps, then 3 deep breaths with your shoulders dropped.

Do it twice a day if it's bad — morning and evening. The other half of it is desk setup: screen at eye level, and get up every 40 minutes or so.

If you get numbness, tingling or shooting pain down the arm, that's not a stretching problem — say so and I'll point you elsewhere.

