2027-01-04T15:45:16  thought   They asked for the gist, so keep it tight, not a lecture. Add the one thing that actually matters in practice — the contribution limit and that withdrawals are tax-free after 59½ — since that's the point of the account.

2027-01-04T15:45:16  message   To the owner:
Retirement account where you pay the tax going in, not coming out.

You put money in after tax is already taken. It grows tax-free, and when you take it out in retirement it's tax-free too — after age 59½ and once the account has been open five years. Take earnings out early and you owe income tax plus a 10% penalty; your contributions you can always pull out penalty-free.

Why people like it: if you expect your tax rate to be the same or higher later, paying it now is the better deal. Contribute up to the annual limit (it's per-person, and you can still contribute for a year up to the tax deadline the following spring).

Couple of caveats: check whether your income is in the range where direct contributions are limited, and pick an actual investment inside it — cash sitting in a Roth isn't doing anything. Want the 2027 numbers?

