2027-01-04T18:23:00  message   From the owner (sent 2027-01-04T18:22:49+00:00):
Tell me something interesting about octopuses.

2027-01-04T18:23:10  reasoning Reasoned (517 chars).
A casual "tell me something interesting." Give a couple of genuinely good octopus facts, well told, short-ish. No tools needed.

Good facts: three hearts, blue blood (hemocyanin), one heart stops when swimming so they prefer crawling; ~2/3 of neurons in arms, arms can act semi-independently; chromatophores controlled directly by neurons not hormones, color change in milliseconds despite being colourblind; taste with skin/ suckers; can squeeze through any hole bigger than their beak.

Pick 2-3 and tell them well.

2027-01-04T18:23:10  thought   Just a fun one. Pick the genuinely surprising facts and tell them well — no list-dump, keep it short.

2027-01-04T18:23:10  message   To the owner:
They taste with their arms. Each sucker has chemoreceptors, so an octopus touching something is literally tasting it — which is also how it identifies what it's grabbed in murky water or in the dark.

The other one that gets me: about two thirds of an octopus's neurons aren't in its head, they're in the arms. Each arm can solve a fair bit of its own problem — reach, grab, hand off food — and the central brain can be doing something else. It's less one animal with eight limbs than one animal that negotiates with eight.

And it's colourblind. It changes colour in milliseconds with muscles in its skin, matching surroundings it can't see as colour, probably reading brightness and polarisation instead.

Three hearts, by the way, and one of them stops when it swims. That's why they'd rather crawl.

