2027-01-05T03:59:26  message   From the owner (sent 2027-01-05T03:59:13+00:00):
Help me word a text declining a party invite without sounding rude.

2027-01-05T03:59:36  reasoning Reasoned (135 chars).
They want wording for declining a party invite. Give a couple of versions — short, warm, no over-explaining. Also a tip: decline early.

2027-01-05T03:59:36  thought   Two or three options at different warmth levels, plus the one rule that actually makes a decline not rude: say no early, don't over-explain.

2027-01-05T03:59:36  message   To the owner:
Rule that matters more than the words: say no early and don't pile on excuses. One reason, no apology spiral, and if you want to soften it, add something specific for later rather than a vague "next time."

Closest friend, warm:
"Ah, I'm going to have to miss this one — I've got something on that weekend I can't move. Genuinely sorry to miss it. Have a brilliant time and send me photos."

Ordinary friend, simple:
"Thanks for the invite, but I can't make that weekend — hope it's a good one."

If you'd like to see them, add a real alternative:
"Can't do the 14th, but I'd love to catch up properly — are you free the week after?"

If they'll ask why and you don't want to explain, hold the line gently: "Just can't make it, sorry — nothing dramatic." That's a complete answer; you don't owe more.

What not to do: wait until the day before, or say "maybe" when you mean no. That's what actually annoys people.

