2027-01-05T04:02:32  message   From the owner (sent 2027-01-05T04:02:26+00:00):
Is there a word for the smell of rain on dry ground?

2027-01-05T04:02:42  reasoning Reasoned (25 chars).
Simple answer: petrichor.

2027-01-05T04:02:42  thought   Easy one: petrichor. Add the mechanism briefly — geosmin, and why our noses are so good at it.

2027-01-05T04:02:42  message   To the owner:
Petrichor. Coined in 1964 by two Australian researchers, from the Greek for stone and the fluid that flows in the veins of the gods.

The smell itself is mostly geosmin, made by soil bacteria, plus some plant oils that build up on rocks and soil during dry spells. Geosmin is released into the air by the first drops hitting the ground — and it's actually more noticeable when rain is light or the ground is warm and dry, which is why the first shower after a dry spell smells stronger than a long one.

Our noses are absurdly good at it. People can detect geosmin at around five parts per trillion — a few drops in an Olympic swimming pool. Nobody's quite sure why we're that sensitive; the leading guess is that our ancestors needed to find water.

