2027-01-05T04:46:10  message   From the owner (sent 2027-01-05T04:46:07+00:00):
Is it worth learning to touch type at my age? Be honest.

2027-01-05T04:46:20  reasoning Reasoned (670 chars).
They asked "at my age" — I don't know their age. Be honest about that, but answer the question properly anyway.

Key honest points: touch typing is worth learning but not for raw speed; most people plateau; the real gain is not looking at keys (frees attention). At any adult age it's learnable — motor learning doesn't stop. Realistic: a few weeks of deliberate practice, ~15-20 min a day, and expect a dip in speed first. Also worth saying: if they're already decent at 50-60 wpm, the return is small. And there's a case that it's not the highest-value skill — dictation exists.

Keep it honest and short-ish. No files needed.

It's 11:46pm — keep it reasonably brief.

2027-01-05T04:46:20  thought   Don't know their age, so be honest about that rather than flattering. The real answer: yes, learnable at any adult age, but the payoff is 'not looking at the keys' more than speed, and only worth it if they're actually slow now. Mention the speed dip, realistic timeline, and the alternative (dictation). Late hour, so keep it tight.

2027-01-05T04:46:20  message   To the owner:
Honest answer: yes, but not for the reason people usually give.

I don't know how old you are, so take the age part as general. Adults learn motor skills more slowly than kids and to the same ceiling — the thing that stops people isn't age, it's that they quit during the awkward phase, which arrives around week two and lasts longer the older you are. Nothing about your hands stops working.

The real gain isn't speed. It's not looking down. Most hunt-and-peck typists top out around 30–40 words a minute and, more importantly, they lose their thread every time they glance at the keyboard. Typing without looking means your eyes stay on the thought. That's worth more than 20 extra words a minute.

When it isn't worth it: if you're already at 50-plus and comfortable, the return is small. And if your bottleneck is writing rather than typing, dictation gets you most of the way for no practice at all.

If you do it: 15 minutes a day, proper home row, and accept your speed drops before it rises. Six weeks of that and you'll be past where you are now. Skip the online games that score you — they teach you to type fast at nonsense, not to type your own sentences.

