2027-01-05T05:03:43  thought   Twenty minutes, midnight, wants one answer not a menu. Give the single best answer with a concrete plan — the surface you see first when you walk in, and a hard rule for what goes where. Keep it short so they can actually spend the time doing it rather than reading.

2027-01-05T05:03:43  message   To the owner:
One answer: the surface you see when you walk into the room. Not the whole room — one flat surface.

Why that one: tidying a whole room for twenty minutes shows nothing, because you're always in the middle of a pile. Clearing one surface completely gives you a visible result you get to walk past fifty times tomorrow, and that's what makes the rest feel possible.

How to spend the twenty:

0–2 min: clear it completely. Everything goes on the floor. The surface empties first — that's the thing that makes it feel done.

2–12 min: three piles on the floor. Belongs elsewhere in this room. Belongs in another room. Rubbish and recycling. Don't sort within the piles, just triage.

12–16 min: the elsewhere-in-this-room pile gets put away properly. Not "near" its home. In it.

16–19 min: the other-room pile goes into one basket by the door, not carried around now.

19–20 min: wipe the surface. Wipe it last so it actually looks finished.

Afterwards: one rule for the basket — it gets emptied before you go to bed tomorrow. Otherwise you've just moved the pile.

Don't start anything bigger than that with twenty minutes. You'll stop halfway and feel worse than before.

