2027-01-05T13:59:29  message   To the owner:
Red lentil soup, about 30 minutes start to finish. The trick is red lentils — they collapse in 15 minutes, no soaking. Green lentils are the two-hour version.

Serves 4.

1 tbsp olive oil, 1 onion chopped, 2 carrots chopped, 3 garlic cloves sliced. Soften in a big pot, 6–7 minutes, medium heat. Don't brown the garlic.

1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp coriander, ½ tsp smoked paprika, pinch of chilli. Stir in for 30 seconds — this is the step people skip and it's why theirs tastes flat. Blooming the spices in oil is most of the flavour.

1 cup red lentils (rinsed), 4 cups stock or water, 1 tin chopped tomatoes. Boil, then simmer 15–18 minutes until the lentils have fallen apart. Stir now and then; red lentils catch on the bottom.

Finish: juice of half a lemon, salt to taste, black pepper. The lemon at the end is not optional — it's the difference between fine and good.

Optional 2 minutes: a knob of butter or a swirl of olive oil on top, and chopped parsley.

Speed notes: if you have a pressure cooker, 6 minutes at pressure instead of 18, same ingredients. And if you want it thicker, pull out a ladleful, blend it, stir it back — no need to blend the whole pot.

Keeps 4 days in the fridge, thickens as it sits so loosen with stock when reheating.

