2027-01-05T14:25:19  thought   (empty)

2027-01-05T14:25:19  message   To the owner:
Don't rub it, and don't put it in the wash yet — heat sets the stain permanently.

1. **Blot, don't wipe.** Paper towel or a clean cloth, press down, lift. Replace as it soaks. Wiping spreads it.

2. **Salt or cornflour, right now** if the shirt is still wet. Pile it on the stain, leave 15 minutes, brush off. It pulls red wine out of the fibres before it dries. Do this before anything else if the spill is fresh.

3. **Cold water flush.** Hold the stain under cold running water, from the back of the fabric so it pushes the wine back out the way it came in.

4. **The actual remover:** a teaspoon of dish soap plus a tablespoon of white vinegar in a cup of cold water. Dab it on, leave 10 minutes, blot, rinse cold. Dish soap because the colour in red wine is bound to the wine's tannins and needs a surfactant, not just water.

5. **If it's dried on:** make a paste of baking soda and cold water, work it in gently, leave 30 minutes, rinse. Or hydrogen peroxide (3%) on white cotton only — it will bleach colour out of anything else, so test an inside seam first.

6. **Wash only once the stain is gone.** Cold, normal detergent, air dry. If you can still see it when it's dry, go back to step 4 rather than putting it through the dryer.

If it's an old, already-dried stain that's been through a wash, that's the hard case — say so and I'll give you the last-resort options (oxygen bleach soak is usually the one that works).

