2027-01-06T01:53:51  reasoning Reasoned (1984 chars).
The question: pronunciation of "quinoa" — KEEN-wah — and why. Answer: Spanish loanword from Quechua "kinwa"/"kinoa"; Spanish spelling pronunciation would be "kee-NOH-ah", but the accepted English is KEEN-wah, closer to the Quechua. Explain the orthographic mismatch: "qu" = /k/, "i" gives the glide... Actually let me be careful and honest.

Quechua: kinwa (also written quinua in Spanish). Spanish "quinua" → the "qu" before "i" is /k/, "i" = /i/, so /ˈkinwa/ (KEE-nwah). The "o" in the English spelling "quinoa" came in via an intermediate form; the English pronunciation kept the Quechua/Spanish "ee-oo-a" → "wah" glide. Actually KEEN-wah: kinwa → "KEEN-wah" where "nw" is pronounced /nw/... "KEEN-wah" = /ˈkiːnwɑː/ or /kiːˈnoʊə/. Both circulations: many say kee-NOH-uh. Merriam-Webster gives \ˈkēn-ˌwä\ and also \kē-ˈnō-ə\.

So honest answer: KEEN-wah is standard, kee-NOH-uh is also accepted. Why the odd spelling: it came through Spanish from Quechua kinwa; the spelling "quinoa" is an older Spanish/medieval transcription; the pronunciation English uses is closer to the Quechua than the spelling suggests — the "oa" doesn't represent /oʊə/ but the "wa" glide. Note the "qu" = k as in Spanish.

