2027-01-06T19:22:41  message   From the owner (sent 2027-01-06T19:22:31+00:00):
What should I know before buying a used electric kettle? Anything?

2027-01-06T19:22:51  reasoning Reasoned (1055 chars).
They ask about buying a used electric kettle. Give practical checklist. Note model can't see images — irrelevant here. Keep concise but useful.

Points: scale buildup, check inside for scale/corrosion, limescale test, heating element type (concealed flat element vs exposed coil — flat concealed disc easier to clean), cordless base contacts corrosion, spout, lid fit, filter at spout, check for cracks in plastic, water level window, rust in stainless, plastic taste/smell, check cable and plug, boil test at viewing, check thermal cutoff switch, power rating (US 120V, how fast), boil-dry protection, auto shutoff, ensure it's a kettle not recalled model, check handle attachment to body (common failure), price vs new (a basic new kettle is ~$20-25 so don't pay $15 for risky used). Also check hard water area → scale. Also check whether it's been used to boil things other than water (smell). Also don't buy one with a damaged cord — no repair. US note: 120V kettles slower; European 3kW ones via converter are a bad idea.

Keep it tight but thorough.

2027-01-06T19:22:51  thought   (empty)

2027-01-06T19:22:51  message   To the owner:
Yes, a few, and most of them take thirty seconds.

**Check it boils first.** Ask to plug it in. Fill halfway, switch on, time it. A kettle that's slow to boil, clicks off early, or won't shut off by itself isn't worth refurbishing.

**Look inside, not outside.** The outside can be polished; the inside can't be faked. Scale is a white crust — usually harmless and removable with vinegar, but heavy scale means it was used hard in hard water. Scratched, pitted or discoloured metal on the base is different: if it's rust in a stainless kettle, or a flaking non-stick coating, walk away.

**The element type matters for how it lives.** A flat concealed disc set into the base is easy to descale and near-bulletproof. An exposed coil in the water works but crusts up fast and is fiddly to clean. A plastic-bodied kettle with a hidden element is fine if it's clean, but plastic can take on the smell of whatever else was boiled in it — sniff the inside and the lid.

**The two things that actually fail on used kettles:** the contacts on the base, and the handle-to-body joint. Look at the base contacts for corrosion or blackening (that's from water sitting in the base), and check the handle for wobble or a hairline crack — that's the bit that snaps, and it isn't repairable.

**Then the small stuff:** lid that seals and doesn't pop off, spout filter present (or it'll pour half a cup of scale into your tea), water-level window still see-through, no cracks in the plastic near the base or the spout. Check the cord for nicks or a repaired plug — a damaged cord is an instant no, it's not worth rewiring a kettle.

**The economics are the real point.** A plain new kettle is about $20-25. So if the used one is more than half that price, or it's got any of the problems above, the maths doesn't work — you're buying someone else's scale and no warranty. Used is worth it at $5-10 for something clean and demonstrably working, or if it's a genuinely good brand (a used Dualit or Stelton is worth more than a new bargain one).

One warning: if you see a European 3kW kettle sold with a plug converter, don't. It'll trip breakers or blow the plug, and US 120V kettles are slow enough that you'll resent it anyway — if boiling speed matters, a stovetop kettle or a US 1500W model is the honest choice.

