2027-01-07T13:46:32  thought   (empty)

2027-01-07T13:46:32  message   To the owner:
Blot, don't rub — and don't put it anywhere near the wash until the stain's gone, because heat sets red wine for good.

1. If it's still wet, pile salt or cornflour on it now, leave 15 minutes, brush off. That's the best thing you can do in the first five minutes.
2. Flush with cold water from the back of the fabric, so it pushes the wine out the way it came in.
3. Then the real remover: a teaspoon of dish soap plus a tablespoon of white vinegar in a cup of cold water. Dab on, leave 10 minutes, blot, rinse cold. The soap matters — wine colour binds to tannins and needs a surfactant, water alone won't shift it.
4. Dried on? Baking soda paste (cold water), work in gently, 30 minutes, rinse. Hydrogen peroxide 3% works on white cotton only — test an inside seam, it bleaches colour out of anything else.
5. Wash cold, air dry, and only once you can't see the stain. Check before the dryer.

If it's already been through a hot wash and dried, that's the hard case — say so and I'll give you the oxygen-bleach soak method.

