2027-01-07T18:16:20  message   To the owner:
Repeat of Wednesday's, so the short version.

An API is a toaster you operate with a sentence instead of your hands. You don't touch the heating element — you use the slot and the lever. That's the interface: the few parts you're allowed to use, designed so you can operate the toaster without understanding it or setting it on fire.

For software: one program offers another a small set of levers — "give me the weather for this town", "charge this card" — and the other pulls the lever without knowing what's inside.

Three consequences, and they're the whole idea. You can't reach past the lever, which is why it's safe but occasionally too limited. You need to know the lever's name, which is what documentation is. And the toaster can change its insides without you noticing, as long as the slot and lever stay the same — that's why two teams who never speak can build on each other.

