2027-01-07T20:55:04  thought   (empty)

2027-01-07T20:55:04  message   To the owner:
Asked this on Monday, but you asked for the non-obvious version, so here's more than I gave you then.

The 4am part is specific, not random — it's roughly when your core temperature is at its lowest and cortisol starts climbing, so you're already close to the surface. What wakes you is often one of four things, in order of how often they're the answer:

**Alcohol.** Even one drink in the evening suppresses the early deep sleep then rebounds you into lighter sleep around 3–4am. This is the single most common fixed-time waker and nobody believes it about themselves until they test it.

**Blood sugar.** If dinner was early or carb-heavy, you can dip low enough at 4am for adrenaline to pull you up. Test: a spoon of something protein-rich — nuts, yoghurt — before bed for a week. If the 4am wakings stop, it was that. Don't do this if you have any reason to suspect reflux.

**Room temperature.** Core temperature bottoms out around 4am and your body defends it by waking you if the room is too warm. Cooler room, lighter duvet. Cheap test, quick answer.

**A genuine early-morning body clock.** If you get up at 4 and feel fine, and you're sleepy by 8–9pm, this isn't insomnia at all, it's a phase shift — and the fix is light in the evening, not darkness.

The practical move: keep a two-line note by the bed for a week. Time woken, and whether you fell back asleep. If you're reliably awake and alert at 4am you've got the last one; if you're awake and wired, it's more likely the first two. The distinction changes what to do, and it takes a week to know which you are.

