2027-01-09T20:06:46  thought   (empty)

2027-01-09T20:06:46  message   To the owner:
Repeat — short version.

Slipping in one gear under load, with hooked or shark-fin teeth, is the cassette. Slipping randomly across gears is usually a worn chain.

Measure it: a ruler from the first pin should hit the 12th pin at exactly 12 inches. 1/16 over means a new chain; 1/8 over and the cassette is probably gone too — a new chain on a worn cassette can slip worse. Also check for a stiff link (pedal backwards and watch for a kink) and a bent derailleur hanger.

(Second time for this one.)

