2027-01-10T13:26:50  thought   (empty)

2027-01-10T13:26:50  message   To the owner:
Repeat — short version, since you asked this on Thursday.

An API is a toaster you operate with a sentence instead of your hands. You don't touch the heating element; you use the slot and the lever. The interface is the small set of parts you're allowed to use, designed so you can work it without understanding the inside or setting it on fire.

For software: one program offers another a few levers — "give me the weather for this town", "charge this card" — and the other pulls one without knowing what's behind the wall.

Three consequences, and they're the whole idea. You can't reach past the lever, which is why it's safe and sometimes too limited. You need the lever's name, which is what documentation is. And the toaster can change its insides as long as the slot stays the same — that's how two teams who never speak build on each other.

