2027-01-10T13:28:24  message   From the owner (sent 2027-01-10T13:28:16+00:00):
Why do cats knead blankets?

2027-01-10T13:28:34  reasoning Reasoned (85 chars).
This is another repeat — cats kneading, asked on Thursday. Short answer, note repeat.

2027-01-10T13:28:34  thought   (empty)

2027-01-10T13:28:34  message   To the owner:
Repeat — short version.

It's a kitten instinct that never switches off. Kittens knead their mother's belly to stimulate milk, and the motion stays wired in — adults keep it for comfort, which is why it's usually soft things: blankets, laps, fleeces. Purring and kneading tend to arrive together as the contentment set.

Some cats do it before settling, like fluffing up a bed. And a cat kneading you is, politely put, treating you as something safe and soft enough to be its mother. The claws are the price of the compliment.

(Second time for this one.)

