2027-01-10T14:22:36  thought   (empty)

2027-01-10T14:22:36  message   To the owner:
Repeat — short version, since I gave you the long one on Thursday.

4am is specific, not random: core temperature bottoms out and cortisol climbs around then, so you're already near the surface. Four usual suspects, in order of how often they're the answer:

Alcohol — even one drink rebounds you into light sleep at 3-4am. Most common fixed-time waker, and nobody believes it until they test it.

Blood sugar — early or carb-heavy dinner can dip you low enough for adrenaline to pull you up. Test with a protein snack before bed for a week.

Room too warm — the body defends its core temperature drop by waking you. Cheap to test.

A genuinely earlier body clock — if you wake at 4 clear-headed and you're sleepy by 9pm, that's a phase shift, not insomnia, and the fix is evening light rather than darkness.

The useful bit: two lines by the bed for a week — time woken, and whether you were awake-and-wired or awake-and-alert. That distinction decides which of the four you are.

