2027-01-10T15:08:21  message   To the owner:
Repeat, shortest form.

An API is a toaster you work with a sentence instead of your hands: a small set of levers one program offers another — "get the weather for this town", "charge this card" — so a second program can use it without knowing what's inside, or reaching the heating element.

Three consequences: you can't reach past the lever, so it's safe but sometimes too limited; you need the lever's name, which is what documentation is; and the insides can change as long as the slot stays the same. That last one is why two teams who never speak can build on each other.

(Fourth time for this one.)

