Repeat — short version.

It's a kitten instinct that never switches off. Kittens knead their mother's belly to stimulate milk, and the motion stays wired in; adults keep it for comfort, which is why it lands on soft things — blankets, laps, fleeces. Purring and kneading arrive together as the contentment set.

Some cats do it before settling, like fluffing up a bed. And a cat kneading you is, politely put, treating you as something safe and soft enough to be its mother. The claws are the price of the compliment.

(Third time for this one.)

