2027-01-10T22:07:23  thought   (empty)

2027-01-10T22:07:23  message   To the owner:
Repeat — short version, fourth time.

4am is specific rather than random: your core temperature bottoms out and cortisol climbs around then, so you're already near the surface and anything mildly disruptive tips you over. Four usual suspects, in order of how often they're the answer:

Alcohol — even one drink rebounds you into light sleep at 3-4am. Most common fixed-time waker, and almost nobody believes it until they test it.

Blood sugar — a light or carb-heavy dinner can dip you low enough for adrenaline to pull you up. Try a protein snack before bed for a week.

Room too warm — the body defends its overnight temperature drop by waking you.

A genuinely earlier body clock — if you wake at 4 clear-headed and feel sleepy by 9pm, that's a phase shift, not insomnia, and the fix is evening light rather than darkness.

The useful bit: two lines by the bed for a week — time woken, and whether you were wired or just alert. That distinction picks which of the four you are.

